.TH OB-FP-SUBMIT 8 "September 2026" "open-bastion" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
ob-fp-submit \- deposit an SSH key fingerprint with ob-fp-daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B ob-fp-submit
.RI "< " request
.SH DESCRIPTION
The unprivileged half of the SSH fingerprint spool, in the same shape as
.BR ob-cert-request (1)
is for
.BR ob-cert-daemon (8).
.PP
The
.B AuthorizedPrincipalsCommand
helper
.RB ( ob-ssh-principals )
runs as the unprivileged
.B AuthorizedPrincipalsCommandUser
and, since issue #249, no longer writes
\fI/run/open\-bastion/ssh\-fp/\fR itself. It pipes the fingerprint here instead,
and this connects to
.I /run/open-bastion/ssh-fp.sock
where
.BR ob-fp-daemon (8)
writes the drop as root.
.PP
There is deliberately nothing to configure and no identity to assert. The
daemon takes the depositing uid from
.B SO_PEERCRED
and derives the sshd anchor from this process's own
.I /proc
ancestry, so a caller cannot name the session it is writing for. That is why
running this binary is harmless, and why it is
.B not
setuid and needs no privilege of its own.
.SH INPUT
Three newline-delimited lines on standard input:
.TP
.B line 1
the fingerprint, \fBSHA256:<base64>\fR
.TP
.B line 2
the key algorithm (sshd's \fB%t\fR, may be empty)
.TP
.B line 3
the key blob (sshd's \fB%k\fR, may be empty)
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
the daemon accepted the deposit
.TP
.B 1
the daemon refused it, or the socket is unreachable
.TP
.B 2
usage or local error
.PP
Callers must treat every non-zero status the same way and
.B carry on.
Losing the binding removes an additional check; it must never fail an
authentication that sshd has already accepted.
.BR pam_openbastion (8)
reports the missing drop itself (issue #192).
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B OB_FP_SOCKET
Overrides the socket path. For the test suite only \(em on a real host the
daemon's socket is the one systemd created, and pointing this elsewhere simply
means no drop is written.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR ob-fp-daemon (8),
.BR ob-cert-request (1),
.BR ob-bastion-setup (8),
.BR ob-backend-setup (8)
.SH AUTHOR
Linagora
